WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Heap allocation is useful when an object's size, lifetime, or ownership pattern does not fit a simple local scope.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:15.560
Variable-size data, long-lived objects, polymorphic objects, and shared structures may all justify dynamic storage.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:23.660
The safety requirement is that heap use must communicate who owns the object, who may borrow it, who may transfer it, and when it is destroyed.

4
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.940
Unclear ownership is where heap use becomes fragile.

5
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.600
If no owner is responsible for cleanup, the program leaks memory or resources.

6
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.580
If two pieces of code believe they own the same object, double cleanup can occur.

7
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.080
If code keeps a raw pointer after the owner destroys or moves the object, stale access becomes possible.

8
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:50.820
These defects are difficult to review because the allocation, transfer, use, and release may be far apart.

9
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.900
Modern C++ provides better patterns than raw owning pointers.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.200
A factory can return a std::unique_ptr to make exclusive ownership explicit.

11
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.340
A class can store ownership as a member so destruction follows object lifetime.

12
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:14.520
Shared ownership should be represented intentionally, not used as a shortcut for avoiding ownership design.

13
00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:24.460
When code only needs temporary access, references, raw non-owning pointers, spans, or views may be appropriate if their lifetimes are clear.

14
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.180
Allocation and cleanup responsibilities should match visibly.

15
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.400
Avoid APIs where callers must guess whether they should delete a returned pointer.

16
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.700
Avoid mixing allocation styles without clear wrappers.

17
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:46.480
During modernization, prioritize raw owning pointers and manual delete paths because they are often where leaks, double cleanup, and stale access begin.

18
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.720
Good heap discipline also includes failure behavior.

19
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:55.660
Allocation can fail, construction can throw, and later setup can discover invalid input.

20
00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:03.200
RAII-based owners make these paths easier because partially completed work is cleaned up through object lifetime.

21
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:11.060
If code allocates first and then relies on several manual cleanup branches, every future edit must preserve that fragile sequence.

22
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.760
A clear owner gives reviewers one place to verify destruction.
